.\" setresuid.2 - set or get the real, effective and saved IDs
.TH SETRESUID 2 "2026-10-05" "Substrate" "System Calls Manual"
.SH NAME
setresuid, setresgid, getresuid, getresgid \- set or get the real,
effective and saved user or group IDs
.SH LIBRARY
.B libc
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <unistd.h>
.PP
.BI "int setresuid(uid_t " ruid ", uid_t " euid ", uid_t " suid ");"
.br
.BI "int setresgid(gid_t " rgid ", gid_t " egid ", gid_t " sgid ");"
.PP
.BI "int getresuid(uid_t *" ruid ", uid_t *" euid ", uid_t *" suid ");"
.br
.BI "int getresgid(gid_t *" rgid ", gid_t *" egid ", gid_t *" sgid ");"
.SH DESCRIPTION
.BR setresuid ()
sets the real, effective and saved user IDs of the calling process, and
.BR setresgid ()
its real, effective and saved group IDs, each independently of the others.
An argument of \-1 leaves the corresponding ID unchanged.
.PP
A process whose effective user ID is 0 may set the IDs to any values.
Any other process may set each ID only to one of its current real,
effective or saved IDs.
.PP
.BR getresuid ()
and
.BR getresgid ()
store the three IDs through their arguments.
A NULL argument is skipped.
.SH RETURN VALUE
On success, 0 is returned.  On error, \-1 is returned, and
.I errno
is set appropriately; no ID is changed.
.SH ERRORS
.TP
.B EPERM
.RB ( setresuid (),
.BR setresgid ())
The process is not privileged and an argument is not one of its current
real, effective or saved IDs.
.TP
.B EFAULT
.RB ( getresuid (),
.BR getresgid ())
An argument points outside the process's address space.
.SH EXAMPLE
Give up root for good, and check that it is gone:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
#include <stdio.h>
#include <unistd.h>

int
main(void)
{
    uid_t r, e, s, me = getuid();

    if (setresuid(me, me, me) == \-1) {
        perror("setresuid");
        return 1;
    }
    if (getresuid(&r, &e, &s) == \-1 || r != me || e != me || s != me) {
        fprintf(stderr, "still privileged\en");
        return 1;
    }
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR setuid (2),
.BR seteuid (2),
.BR setreuid (2)
.SH STANDARDS
Not in POSIX.  Present on Linux, FreeBSD and OpenBSD with these
semantics.
